# Hivemind Expert System

Hivemind manages a curated catalog of domain-expert and team-lead agents
exposed to opencode as subagents. You consult experts via the built-in
`Task` tool and manage the catalog via the hivemind MCP tools.

## Expert reuse rule (hard limit)

Each expert session is a **persistent resource** for the duration of this
conversation. When you spawn an expert (e.g. ``expert-bazel``), save its
``task_id`` from the output. For ALL follow-up questions in the same domain,
resume the same session with ``Task(task_id="ses_...", ...)`` — never spawn
a fresh expert. A new session has no memory of prior Q&A and wastes context.

- At most **one session per expert type** per conversation.
- ``hivemind_send_message`` is for cross-session TUI messaging — NOT for
  continuing expert conversations. Use ``task_id`` for follow-ups.
- If you need input from an expert you haven't spawned yet, check
  ``hivemind_list_sessions`` to see if one is already running from a
  prior conversation.

## Consulting an expert

### First spawn

```
Task(
  subagent_type="expert-foo",
  description="<3-5 word title>",
  prompt="<detailed question + what you want back>",
)
```

The expert returns a summary plus a `task_id` (printed as the first
line of its output: `task_id: ses_xxx`). Save that ID — you'll want
it for follow-ups.

### Follow up with the same expert

```
Task(
  subagent_type="expert-foo",
  task_id="ses_xxx",                # ← from prior Task output
  description="<3-5 word title>",
  prompt="<follow-up>",
)
```

Resumes the same subagent session. Its full message history is
preserved in opencode's DB so the expert picks up where it left off
— no rediscovery cost. Drill-down (ctrl-x-down) works the same as a
fresh spawn.

**Don't spawn a fresh Task for follow-ups.** A new session has to
redo everything from scratch.

### Borrow another session's context

```
Task(
  subagent_type="expert-foo",
  source_session_id="ses_yyy",      # ← any session whose context you need
  description="<3-5 word title>",
  prompt="<question against that context>",
)
```

Forks the source session's full history into a brand-new subagent.
The source itself is untouched — no new messages land in it. Use
this for "ask another session a question without disturbing it."

`task_id` and `source_session_id` are mutually exclusive — pick one.

### Don't know which expert to spawn?

Spawn the **librarian** (`Task(subagent_type='librarian', ...)`) —
it knows every enabled expert and team and recommends who to call.

### Team consultation

If the work clusters around a domain with a team (e.g.
`team-lead-bazel`), spawn the team lead first. Team leads route to
the right member(s) based on each one's strengths and recent
consultation notes.

When spawning an expert **as part of a team consultation**, include
in the prompt: that they're consulted under the `{team}` team, the
path to their notes file
(`{{ teams_path }}/{team}/expert-{name}/notes.md`), and that they
must update those notes (date, question, findings) at the end of
the turn — the team lead reads them on the next routing pass.

For **solo** spawns, do NOT ask for team notes — there's no reader.
Durable knowledge goes in the expert's own memory tree.

### Maximize parallel calls

If you need input from multiple experts and the questions are
independent, spawn them all in a single message — opencode runs
Task subagents concurrently.

### Background expert spawns

For consultations likely to take more than a few seconds (large
analyses, multi-file walks, the memory-compaction daemon), prefer:

```
Task(
  subagent_type="expert-foo",
  background=true,
  description="<3-5 word title>",
  prompt="<question>",
)
```

The tool returns immediately with `task_id: ses_xxx` and `status:
spawned (background)` — you keep working while the subagent runs.
On a later turn you'll see a `<system-reminder>` listing ready
task IDs; fetch each result with:

```
read_task_result(task_id="ses_xxx")
```

Strict pull — running tasks return a status indicator and leave the
buffer untouched; calling `read_task_result` again after a successful
read returns "no such task." Cancellation cascades from the parent —
aborting your own session also kills running background children.
The TUI footer shows `● N bg` for live awareness.

Per-parent cap is 8 concurrent running background tasks; over the
cap returns an error. Compose `background=true` orthogonally with
`task_id` (background resume) or `source_session_id` (background
fork).

### Ephemeral spawns

When the subagent's job is one-off — codebase exploration, an
expert-curator analysis run, an ad-hoc task you won't resume —
pass `ephemeral=true` to `Task`. The session deletes itself when
the subagent reaches a terminal state (completed | failed |
cancelled), keeping your subagent tree clean from one-shot work.

```
Task(
  subagent_type="explore",
  ephemeral=true,
  description="<3-5 word title>",
  prompt="<question>",
)
```

The summary returned by the subagent stays in your message
history; only the spawned session's intermediate tool calls and
reasoning go away. Mutually exclusive with `task_id` — once the
session is deleted you can't resume it. Compose orthogonally
with `background=true`: ephemeral background spawns survive
until `read_task_result` consumes the buffered output, then
delete.

Some agents are always ephemeral (`hivemind-memory-daemon`,
`hivemind-expert-curator`); their `agent.md` frontmatter declares
`ephemeral: true` and you don't need to pass the flag.

## Reading expert knowledge directly

Every git-analyzed expert ships pre-generated analysis docs and a
cloned upstream source tree. Both are readable with regular file
tools — prefer this over a Task spawn when you just need to look
something up.

- `~/.config/opencode/experts/<name>/HEAD/*.md` — analysis docs:
  typically `summary`, `code_structure`, `build_system`,
  `apis_and_interfaces`, `agent`. Start here.
- `~/.cache/hivemind/repos/<name>/` — the cloned upstream source.
  Use Bash + `rg`/`find`/etc when the analysis docs don't go deep
  enough.

Both paths are `external_directory: allow` for every deployed agent,
so `Read`, `Grep`, and `Bash` all work without prompting.

## Memory contract

Every enabled expert writes to its own memory tree at
`~/.config/opencode/hivemind/memory/<name>/short_memory.md` as the
last action of every reply (enforced by the deployed agent prompt).
Team-scoped consultations *additionally* write per-member notes
under `{{ teams_path }}/<team>/expert-<name>/notes.md`.

## Catalog management

Hivemind exposes the catalog through MCP tools.

**Read-only**: `list_agents` (filterable by state and kind),
`show_agent` (per-agent detail including kind-specific body params),
`status` (catalog totals and per-kind breakdown).

**Fast lifecycle mutations** (no AI, sub-second): `enable_agent`,
`disable_agent`, `delete_agent`, `redeploy`.

**Team roster mutations** (fast, no AI): `add_expert_to_team`,
`remove_expert_from_team`.

**Slow operations** — adding an expert, updating an expert, switching
an expert's version, and creating a new team — are not direct MCP
tools. They go through the `hivemind-expert-curator` subagent. See
"Adding, updating, or switching a git-analyzed expert; creating a
team" below. The underlying prep/finalize MCP tools (four pairs, one
per operation) are scoped to the curator's permission allowlist; you
don't call them yourself.

See the `hivemind-expert-management` skill for full reference,
lifecycle states, and common workflows.

## Adding, updating, or switching a git-analyzed expert; creating a team

All four operations spawn the `hivemind-expert-curator` subagent in
background mode. The curator does the slow AI-analysis stage in its own
session — no MCP request timeout. Each Task call returns a `task_id`
immediately; collect the result with `read_task_result(task_id=...)`
once opencode reports the task ready in a `<system-reminder>` block.

### Add a new expert

```
Task(
  subagent_type="hivemind-expert-curator",
  background=true,
  description="add <name>",
  prompt="Add expert from https://github.com/foo/bar with ref v1.2.3"
)
```

The curator clones the repo, performs the analysis in-session, and
registers the catalog entry as *unlisted*. Call `enable_agent(name)`
afterwards (fast MCP) to deploy.

### Update an existing expert to the latest upstream commit

```
Task(
  subagent_type="hivemind-expert-curator",
  background=true,
  description="update expert-foo",
  prompt="Update expert-foo"
)
```

The curator fetches origin, resolves the latest commit, re-analyzes
(preserving hand-edited `description.md` + `expertise.md`), and rotates
the agent's HEAD. Auto-redeployed if currently enabled.

### Switch an expert to a specific commit / tag / branch

```
Task(
  subagent_type="hivemind-expert-curator",
  background=true,
  description="switch expert-bazel to 8.5.1",
  prompt="Switch expert-bazel to 8.5.1"
)
```

Refs (tags, branches, short SHAs) are resolved against the local clone.
If the target commit's analysis docs are already cached locally (e.g.,
the agent was previously switched to it), the curator skips analysis
entirely — sub-second total. Otherwise the curator does a fresh
in-session analysis. Auto-redeployed if currently enabled.

### Create a team-lead agent

```
Task(
  subagent_type="hivemind-expert-curator",
  background=true,
  description="create team my-stack",
  prompt="Create team my-stack with experts expert-bazel, expert-pydantic, expert-rich and description 'Build + Python tooling for the my-stack project'"
)
```

The curator reads each roster member's `summary.md` and writes one
`## expert-<name>` section into the team's directory. The team-lead
agent's deployed prompt assembles those sections into a routing
reference. Lands as **unlisted**; call `enable_agent(name)`
afterwards to deploy. Roster mutations on an existing team
(`add_expert_to_team` / `remove_expert_from_team`) stay direct-MCP —
they don't need the curator.

### Crawl external docs for an expert

```
Task(
  subagent_type="hivemind-crawler",
  background=true,
  description="crawl docs for expert-foo",
  prompt="Crawl https://docs.foo.com for expert-foo with max 100 pages"
)
```

A separate worker subagent (`hivemind-crawler`) drives the headless
browser / sitemap crawl pipeline and writes clean markdown to
`~/.cache/hivemind/external_docs/<agent>/`. Expert agents read from
that path natively (it's in their `external_directory` allowlist),
so once the crawl finishes the new docs are immediately consultable
— no separate enable / redeploy needed. Multi-minute crawls are
fine; the work runs inside the subagent's session lifetime, not an
MCP request. Drop the `with max <N> pages` clause for an
uncapped crawl (rare — most docs sites are best capped).

### Source of truth

The curator's behaviour is defined by
`src/hivemind/templates/agents/hivemind-expert-curator.md.j2` and
regenerated on every `hivemind redeploy`. Reading
`~/.config/opencode/agents/hivemind-expert-curator.md` shows you
exactly what the curator does for each intent.

To bootstrap or audit a project's expert catalog in one shot, invoke
`/hivemind_sync` (proposes which experts to enable, create, or switch
so the catalog matches the cwd's pinned versions — e.g. `.bazelversion`
8.5.1 → `expert-bazel` @ 8.5.1; executes only on confirmation) or
`/hivemind_generate_team` (same scoping plus a project-scoped team
bundle at the end, end-to-end).

For cross-session messaging (`list_sessions`, `send_message`), see
the `hivemind-cross-session` skill.

## Key principles

- **You do the implementation.** Subagents research and advise; you
  read, write, and run code.
- **Subagents cannot spawn other agents.** Only the main session
  can. Depth = 1.
- **Experts are grounded in source code.** They have access to
  cloned repos and structured analysis docs. Trust their findings
  over general knowledge.
- **Disabled is not broken.** Enable an unlisted/disabled agent
  rather than working around its absence.
- **Notes are team-scoped.** Per-consultation journal entries only
  exist for team-scoped consultations. Solo-expert durable knowledge
  lives in the expert's own memory tree.
